WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 225: Austria: The Habsburg Double Eagle, Franz Joseph, and the Ballrooms of Vienna

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:22.880
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the gaslit boulevards, grand opera houses, and shadowy imperial chambers of Findicycle Vienna to witness the golden autumn and tragic collapse of one of history's oldest dynasties, the Austro-Hungarian Empire of Emperor Franz Joseph.

3
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:35.840
[George] In the spring of 1848, a fiery wave of popular revolutions swept across the European continent, threatening to tear the multi-ethnic Habsburg Empire apart.

4
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:43.520
[George] In Vienna, students and workers built barricades, in Budapest, Hungarian patriots proclaimed a free republic.

5
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.360
[George] In northern Italy, Milan and Venice rose in revolt.

6
00:00:47.360 --> 00:01:00.560
[George] Amidst this chaos, the feeble-minded Emperor Ferdinand was persuaded to abdicate, and on December 2, 1848, his 18-year-old nephew, Franz Joseph, was crowned Emperor of Austria.

7
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.720
[George] Possessed of iron military discipline and a Spartan work ethic.

8
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:19.680
[George] Franz Joseph arose at 4 o'clock every morning throughout his entire life, sleeping on an iron military camp bed, dressing in a simple field marshal's uniform, and spending 12 hours a day signing state documents with meticulous precision.

9
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:31.840
[George] Aided by Russian military intervention in Hungary and the brilliant military generalship of Field Marshal Raditzky in Italy, young Franz Joseph crushed the revolts and restored imperial order.

10
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:40.160
[George] Embarking upon a marathon's 68-year reign that would see the transition of Europe from horse-drawn carriages to airplanes and machine guns.

11
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:54.080
[George] Young Franz Joseph was groomed from early childhood by his ambitious mother, Archduchess Sophie, who instilled in him an unshakable conviction that the Habsburg dynasty was divinely ordained to rule over the multi-ethnic empire.

12
00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:04.680
[George] Field Marshal Raditzky's decisive victories at Costoza and Novara in northern Italy saved the Habsburg Empire in 1848, inspiring Johann Strauss S.R.

13
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:10.360
[George] to compose the immortal Raditsky March in celebration of Austrian military triumph.

14
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:21.160
[George] In December 1857, Franz Joseph issued a decree that transformed Vienna from a cramped medieval fortress into the most breathtaking modern metropolis in Europe.

15
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:30.680
[George] He ordered the tearing down of the ancient brick city walls and glacis that had defended the city against Ottoman sieges, creating the magnificent Ringstras.

16
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:37.640
[George] This grand, sweeping boulevard was flanked by colossal architectural masterpieces designed in historicist styles.

17
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:49.000
[George] The NeoGothic Rat House, the neoclassical parliament with its Statue of Athena, the Imperial Bergheater, the Grand State Opera, and the Palatial Museum of Art History.

18
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] Wealthy Jewish bankers, industrialists and aristocrats built opulent private palaces along the ring, while treelined promenades were filled with horse-drawn carriages and strolling citizens.

19
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:16.1000
[George] The Ringstrass became the living stage for Vienna's cultural golden age, where the coffeehouse culture flourished, newspaper vendors shouted the evening headlines, and intellectual debate buzzed over Soccer Tort and Melange coffee in legendary cafes like Central and Spurl.

20
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
[George] The Ringstrass was designed with exceptional military foresight.

21
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:31.960
[George] Its broad, open boulevards were intentionally engineered wide enough to prevent barricades from blocking the streets and to allow cavalry and artillery to deploy swiftly from the central barracks.

22
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:47.440
[George] The Vienna State Opera on the Ringstrass opened in May 1869 with a performance of Mozart's Don Giovanni, attended by Franz Joseph and Sissy, inaugurating an era of opera production that set the gold standard for European musical performance.

23
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:55.760
[George] In 1854, Franz Joseph married the 16-year-old Bavarian Princess Elizabeth, universally known as Sissy.

24
00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:12.240
[George] Possessed of legendary beauty, ankle-length chestnut hair that took three hours daily to groom, and a fierce, restless spirit, Sissy hated the suffocating Spanish court etiquette of the Vienna Hofburg, dominated by her domineering mother-in-law Archduchess Sophie.

25
00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:25.120
[George] Sissy was an exceptional equestrian who spent hours riding at breakneck speed across the countryside, starving herself with fanatical diets and exercising on gymnastics rings installed in her imperial dressing room.

26
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:36.480
[George] She felt a deep, instinctive empathy for the Hungarian people, mastering their language and using her personal charm to persuade Franz Joseph to grant Hungary equal partnership in the empire.

27
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:46.800
[George] Increasingly estranged from the court, Sissy spent decades wandering across Europe, Madeira, and Corfu aboard royal yachts, fleeing imperial duties.

28
00:04:46.800 --> 00:05:02.400
[George] Her tragic life ended on September 10, 1898, on the shores of Lake Geneva, when an Italian anarchist named Luigi Lucini plunged a sharpened needle file into her heart, shocking the emperor and turning Sissy into an immortal romantic legend.

29
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.480
[George] Sissy's obsession with her youth and physical appearance was legendary.

30
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:16.960
[George] She slept with raw veal masks on her face, bathed in warm olive oil, and walked 10 to 15 miles every day at a brisk pace that exhausted her ladies in waiting.

31
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:28.880
[George] Sissy's beauty routine was so demanding that she maintained a strict weight of 110 pounds at five feet eight inches tall, lacing her corsets to a waist measurement of just 19 and a half inches.

32
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:41.480
[George] Following devastating military defeats against France in Italy in 1859 and Prussia at the Battle of Kernigretz in 1866, Austria was permanently expelled from both Germany and Italy.

33
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:53.320
[George] Confronted with the rising national aspirations of his diverse subjects, Franz Joseph made a monumental constitutional breakthrough, the Osgleich, or Compromise of 1867.

34
00:05:53.320 --> 00:06:06.040
[George] The agreement transformed the Austrian Empire into the dual monarchy of Austria-Hungary, two sovereign states united under a single monarch, sharing common ministries of foreign affairs, war and finance.

35
00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] In June 1867, Franz Joseph and Sissy were crowned king and queen of Hungary in the Matthias Church in Buda amidst rapturous national celebrations.

36
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.440
[George] Yet this fragile compromise contained the seeds of future ruin.

37
00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:41.960
[George] While the German-speaking Austrians and Magyar Hungarians enjoyed political supremacy, nine other distinct nationalities, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ukrainians, Croats, Serbs, Slovenes, Romanians and Italians, felt marginalized and disenfranchised, turning the Habsburg realm into a ticking ethnic powder keg.

38
00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:57.560
[George] The coronation of Franz Joseph as King of Hungary in 1867 was accompanied by a royal amnesty for all former 1848 revolutionaries, as the crown of Saint Stephen was placed upon his brow to the strains of Liszt's Hungarian coronation mass.

39
00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:09.960
[George] The dual monarchy established separate parliaments in Vienna and Budapest, requiring intricate budgetary negotiations every ten years that frequently threatened to paralyze the joint governance of the empire.

40
00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] While political tensions simmered beneath the surface, late 19th century Vienna was the cultural and intellectual epicenter of the Western world.

41
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:34.720
[George] In the gilded ballrooms of the Hofburg and the Musikvarine, the sparkling waltz of Johann Strauss Jr., including the Blue Danube and Tales from the Vienna Woods, captivated millions, turning the three-quarter time rhythm into the heartbeat of an empire.

42
00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.560
[George] In music, Johannes Brahms, Anton Bruckner, and Gustav Mahler pushed orchestral composition to sublime heights.

43
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:49.920
[George] In painting, Gustav Klimt and Egon Skiel shattered Victorian taboos with the dazzling, sensual Vienna secession.

44
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:57.120
[George] In medicine and psychology, Sigmund Freud unraveled the unconscious mind on the couch of Burgess 19.

45
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:12.320
[George] It was a world of brilliant contradictions, an ancient, conservative Catholic monarchy presiding over the birth of modern psychoanalysis, modern art and modern philosophy, celebrating life with champagne and waltzes on the edge of an abyss.

46
00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:26.720
[George] In the smoky coffeehouses of Vienna, writers like Stefan Zweig and Arthur Schnitzler gathered to read foreign newspapers on wooden rods, composing masterpieces of literature while debating art and politics into the early hours of mourning.

47
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:40.400
[George] The Musikvarine's Golden Hall, opened in 1870, was celebrated for its peerless acoustics, becoming the home of the Vienna Philharmonic Orchestra and the stage for the annual New Year's concert that continues to this day.

48
00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.360
[George] The personal tragedies of France Joseph's reign were Shakespearean in their cruelty.

49
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.920
[George] In 1867, his beloved brother Maximilian was executed by a firing squad in Mexico.

50
00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] In 1898, his wife Sissy was assassinated.

51
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:05.160
[George] But the most devastating blow to the empire occurred on January 30, 1889, at the Imperial Hunting Lodge of Mayerling in the Vienna woods.

52
00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:23.400
[George] There, the emperor's only son and heir, 30-year-old Crown Prince Rudolf, a liberal, brooding intellectual who felt suffocated by his father's conservatism, was found dead alongside his 17-year-old mistress, Baroness Mary Vetsera, in a pact of murder and suicide.

53
00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:34.360
[George] The Mayerling tragedy left Franz Joseph without a direct son to inherit the throne, throwing the imperial succession into the hands of his nephew, Archduke Franz Ferdinand.

54
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:48.920
[George] Franz Ferdinand was an abrasive, deeply controversial figure who married a Czech countess, Sophie Chotek, below his royal rank, further straining family ties and casting a dark shadow over the future of the Habsburg crown.

55
00:09:48.920 --> 00:10:01.640
[George] The suicide of Crown Prince Rudolf at Mayerling sent tremors through the monarchy, leading court officials to cover up the scandal by claiming the prince had died of heart failure before admitting the tragic murder suicide pact.

56
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:14.360
[George] Mary Vetsera's family was forbidden from attending her secret funeral at the monastery of Hyligencruz, where the young Baroness was buried in an unmarked grave wrapped in a carriage blanket to silence royal scandal.

57
00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:23.800
[George] On the bright morning of June 28, 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife Sophie visited the Bosnian city of Sarajevo.

58
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:37.240
[George] As their open-topped car turned onto France Joseph Street, a 19-year-old Serbian nationalist named Gavrillo Princip stepped forward and fired two shots from a Browning pistol, killing both the heir and his wife.

59
00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:49.920
[George] The assassination activated Europe's tangled network of military alliances, dragging Austria-Hungary, Germany, Russia, France, and Britain into the catastrophic inferno of the First World War.

60
00:10:49.920 --> 00:11:03.760
[George] In November 1916, amidst the muddy slaughter of the Eastern Front, the 86-year-old Emperor Franz Joseph passed away peacefully in Schoenbrunn Palace, having outlived his wife, his son, and his heir.

61
00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:12.240
[George] Two years later, in November 1918, the Austro-Hungarian Empire disintegrated into half a dozen independent nations.

62
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:24.400
[George] After 640 years of continuous rule, the Habsburg Double Eagle fell from the ramparts, bringing to a close an era of elegance, tragedy, and imperial grandeur that would never return.

63
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:37.360
[George] When Franz Joseph received the news of Franz Ferdinand's assassination at Sarajevo, the 83-year-old emperor bowed his head and whispered, The Almighty does not allow himself to be challenged with impunity.

64
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.680
[George] A higher power has restored the order that I was unable to maintain.

65
00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:53.200
[George] The collapse of the Austro-Hungarian Empire in 1918 dissolved a dynamic multi-ethnic realm of 52 million people, leaving Vienna an imperial capital without an empire.

66
00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.200
[George] Surrounded by the nascent nation-states of Central Europe.

67
00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:14.320
[Rebecca] Sweden Part 1 Gustavus Adolphus, the Lion of the North, and the Battle of Breitenfeld.

68
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.800
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

